iclr-2026-0111e4fa230c,ICLR,oral,replication_200,table,experimental_design,main,Table 1,5,"Compares problem source, natural/programming language counts, instruction/context length, highlight support, and test cases across edit benchmarks.",inset,223.29|194.20|408.17|237.13,table,1,2,1,"Table 1: Comparing EDIT-Bench to other edit-related benchmarks. We compare EDIT-Bench with similar benchmarks (CanItEdit (Cassano et al., 2023b), EditEval (Hu et al., 2023), Aider Polyglot) in terms of the problem source, user instruction (# NL refers to the number of natural languages), code context (# PL refers to the number of programming languages, HL refers to whether users can highlight a subset of code), and associated test cases. Standard deviation is indicated by ±. EDIT-Bench is the only benchmark built from in-the-wild problems and exhibits considerable variation in both instruction and code context length.",92,4,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusMonL-Regu|NimbusMonL-Bold|CMSY10,9.963,1,1,0,1,1,0,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|CMSY9,7.13,7.922,7.922,grayscale,0,,0.0,2,2,2
iclr-2026-0111e4fa230c,ICLR,oral,replication_200,table,experimental_design,main,Table 2,6,Contrasts real IDE instructions and annotator-written CanItEdit/EditEval prompts over four task categories.,double_column,89.69|148.48|522.00|399.92,table,1,1,1,"Table 2: Comparing user instructions written in IDE to the instructions written by human annotators. We provide examples across different task categories, comparing with two edit-related datasets (CanItEdit (Cassano et al., 2023b) and EditEval (Hu et al., 2023)). We truncate some instructions for brevity and provide full examples in Appendix B. In general, we find that real-world prompts are much less specified and require models to leverage the provided context, compared to existing benchmark prompts.",75,4,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,1,1,0,0,0,1,1,NimbusMonL-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusMonL-Bold,7.472,7.472,8.966,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
