iclr-2026-12331806f03c,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,figure,appendix,appendix,Figure 5,15,"Compares Halton, random, and confidence unmasking schedules on a grid.",double_column,90.00|44.98|523.25|351.86,other,3,2,1,"Figure 5: Comparison of unmasking schedules on a 2D grid. (a) Halton sampling covers space uniformly. (b) Random sampling can leave large areas empty. (c) With confidence-based sampling, new tokens are decoded close to existing ones, leading to high mutual information and a risk of inconsistent generation.",47,4,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,0,0,0,0,1,0,1,CMBX10|CMSSBX10|CMR12|CMMI12,4.788,4.788,6.895,color,7,#8FB032|#5E81B5|#E19C24|#E2E3C9|#C3C38D|#839EC5|#E2A02F,0.3761,,,
iclr-2026-12331806f03c,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 3,16,Reports A100 forward and forward+backward latency/throughput uncertainty.,double_column,89.25|137.52|523.24|388.96,table,1,1,1,"Table 3: Latency and throughput for a single forward+backward pass of the MDLMs and PGMs, computed on a single A100-SXM4-80GB GPU. On LM1B, PGM introduces a negligible overhead over MDLM. On OWT, our PGM with 6 encoder and decoder layers and an embedding dimension of 1024 achieves around 75% of the training throughput of MDLM. Recall that at inference, the same PGM is around 5× faster than MDLM.",69,4,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|CMR10|CMSY10,9.963,0,0,0,1,0,0,1,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|CMR9|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMMI9|CMSY9,5.978,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.016,,,
