iclr-2026-3b8d372687ad,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,figure,introduction,main,Figure 1,2,"Dreamina unseen-domain case contrasts GPT-4o, InternVL3-78B, Veritas without MiPO and Veritas reasoning outputs.",double_column,90.00|20.00|522.00|247.13,qualitative_grid,1,2,1,"Figure 1: Comparison of the detection outputs. InternVL3-78B (Zhu et al., 2025) gets incorrect answer. GPT-4o (Hurst et al., 2024) and our model trained without the proposed MiPO both fail to provide precise explanation. In contrast, our model gives transparent and faithful decision process.",44,4,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu,9.963,0,1,0,0,0,0,1,ComicSansMS|NimbusRomNo9L-Regu,5.037,5.138,9.963,color,7,#E2F0D9|#FFEFDA|#4E78A1|#CFCDC0|#824848|#8595A2|#E1EFD8,0.6423,,,
iclr-2026-3b8d372687ad,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,figure,experimental_design,main,Figure 2,4,"Four-panel overview of data collection, hierarchical evaluation, HydraFake subsets and SOTA detector performance.",double_column,90.00|20.00|522.00|227.87,pipeline|conceptual_diagram|qualitative_grid|image_montage,6,5,1,"Figure 2: Overview of HydraFake dataset. (a) We carefully collect and reimplement advanced deepfake techniques to construct our HydraFake dataset. Real images are collected from 8 datasets. Fake images are from classic datasets, high-quality public datasets and our self-constructed deepfake data. (b) We introduce a rigorous evaluation protocol. Training data contains abundant samples but limited forgery types. Evaluations are split into four levels. (c) Illustration of the subsets in different evaluation splits. (d) Performance of existing detectors. Most detectors generalize well on Cross- Model setting but perform poorly on Cross-Forgery and Cross-Domain scenarios.",93,10,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMR10,9.963,1,1,0,0,0,0,1,ComicSansMS|Calibri-Bold|NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|Wingdings-Regular|CambriaMath,1.165,3.955,9.963,color,8,#B57D6B|#443936|#717569|#8A4533|#E6D9C5|#E6D1CC|#DEAA89|#94A79E,0.4011,,,
iclr-2026-3b8d372687ad,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,figure,method,main,Figure 3,6,"Two-stage pipeline linking SFT, MiPO, P-GRPO, sampling and pattern-aware rewards.",double_column,90.00|37.86|522.00|344.74,pipeline,2,2,1,"Figure 3: Overview of two-stage training pipeline. (a) For Pattern-Guided Cold-Start, we first employ SFT to internalize thinking patterns. Then we introduce MiPO to facilitate human-aligned reasoning. The MiPO dataset consists of mixed non-preference data, encouraging model to perform precise and fine-grained reasoning. (b) For Pattern-Aware GRPO, we introduce pattern-aware re- ward to incentivize adaptive reasoning ability on pattern granularity, yielding our VERITAS model.",64,5,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,1,1,0,0,0,0,1,ComicSansMS|CambriaMath|Wingdings-Regular,4.626,4.981,9.963,color,8,#FFEFDA|#E3F1D8|#FFEEDA|#868285|#AEBAB3|#763A4C|#FFF3CC|#E7D9C2,0.4692,,,
iclr-2026-3b8d372687ad,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,results,main,Table 1,8,"Main Accuracy comparison across ID, cross-model, cross-forgery and cross-domain.",double_column,109.83|167.84|502.90|175.86,table,1,1,1,"Table 1: Performance comparison (Acc.) on HydraFake dataset. In-domain (ID) results are aver- aged. To ensure fair comparisons with MLLM-based detectors, 1) we exclude ID set in their average results and 2) further restrict the training scope of our method to FF++, StyleGAN, StableDiffusion XL and FFHQ (similar to FFAA), yielding “VERITAS-MINI”. The best results are bolded and second best are underlined. More metrics in Appendix A.5.",68,5,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,0,1,0,1,0,1,2,NimbusRomNo9L-MediItal,6.545,6.545,6.545,color,1,#DCE6FF,0.8924,1,21,1
