iclr-2026-8374ca36d622,ICLR,oral,replication_200,figure,introduction,main,Figure 1,2,"Conceptual three-part diagram: human high/low-level factors; existing SAEs versus T-SAEs; and top active features over spliced Newton's Principia, an MMLU genetics question, and the Bhagavad Gita.",double_column,90.00|20.00|522.02|227.27,conceptual_diagram,1,3,1,"Figure 1: A) Human language production involves high-level features such as semantic content and surrounding context, as well as low-level features such as syntactical requirements and specific word choices. B) While existing SAEs mostly recover syntactic information, T-SAEs balance recovery of semantics, syntax, and context. C) When decomposing a sequence composed of three passages: Newton’s Principia, an MMLU genetics question, and the Bhagavat Gita (in English), T-SAEs (bot- tom) smoothly detect the semantic shifts in the passage, with highly active features strongly corre- lating to the true content of the text, whereas existing SAEs (such as Matryoshka, top), are much noisier, varying on almost a per-token basis, and do not easily depict these shifts.",115,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,0,0,1,0,0,0,1,Inter-Regular|SpaceMono-Regular|Inter-Bold|Inter-SemiBold|NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|SpaceMono-Bold,1.322,3.726,12.953,color,6,#E8F2FF|#FFF8EC|#B18AB6|#9456AB|#ECD6D9|#BFBFCB,0.5698,,,
iclr-2026-8374ca36d622,ICLR,oral,replication_200,figure,results,main,Figure 2,5,"Nine Pythia-160m t-SNE panels: T-SAE high-level, T-SAE low-level, and Matryoshka all features across semantic, contextual, and syntactic labels.",double_column,90.00|20.00|522.02|291.71,other,1,1,1,"Figure 2: TSNE visualizations of Pythia-160m SAE decompositions of MMLU questions, labeled by question category (left), question number (middle column), and token part-of-speech (right). We see that T-SAE high-level features (top) recover semantics and context. The low-level features of T-SAEs (middle row), as well as Matryoshka SAEs (bottom), recover syntactic information.",51,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu,9.963,0,0,1,0,0,0,1,CourierNewPSMT|Inter-Regular|Inter-Medium|NimbusRomNo9L-Regu,0.885,2.195,9.963,color,8,#E5ECF6|#FCF3C1|#C8C0CE|#AC84A2|#C2DEDE|#E8E2E3|#D3EEE4|#FAEFB8,0.6695,,,
iclr-2026-8374ca36d622,ICLR,oral,replication_200,figure,results,main,Figure 3,6,"Gemma2-2b probe accuracy for semantics, context, and syntax at sparsities 1, 5, 10, 20, and Dense, comparing Temporal, Matryoshka, BatchTopK, and model-latent probes.",double_column,90.00|20.00|522.02|180.62,other,1,1,1,"Figure 3: Accuracy of probes trained on SAE decompositions for various SAEs trained on Gemma2- 2b, as well as probes trained directly on model latents (orange), with semantic labels (right), con- textual labels (middle), and syntactic labels (right) with varying levels of probe sparsity (setup from (Kantamneni et al., 2025)). Dense probes are trained on all features.",57,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu,9.963,0,1,1,0,0,0,2,Inter-Regular|NimbusRomNo9L-Regu,2.6,5.257,9.963,color,8,#EE2076|#FBBC04|#448C27|#F1E4BA|#0806FE|#DD56A3|#A2A383|#DDC883,0.4649,,,
