iclr-2026-9731e2f39bcc,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,figure,method,main,Figure 1,3,"LKH-3 re-optimized-edge percentage over iterative search for CVRP2k/CVRP3k, motivating stable segments",double_column,89.69|369.27|523.74|676.15,network,1,2,1,"Figure 1 depicts that iterative search solvers perform redundant searches, reoptimizing only a small portion while many edges remain unchanged, especially in large subtours with high capacity C. Inspired by Morabit et al. (2024), we formally study the decomposition technique, First-Segment- Then-Aggregate (FSTA), for accelerating iterative search solvers. As shown in the top of Figure 2, FSTA segments the VRP solutions by identifying unstable portions, and then groups the stable",70,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-MediItal|CMMI10,9.963,0,0,1,1,0,0,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|CMMI10|CMMI7|CMR10|CMSY10|CMEX10,6.974,9.963,11.955,color,8,#B1D7B0|#0101FB|#007F01|#B7C0E8|#6CA588|#9AC1AE|#2E3ED7|#3E8B61,0.0313,,,
iclr-2026-9731e2f39bcc,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,figure,method,main,Figure 2,4,"Overview of FSTA and the three L2Seg decoding paths, including SYN’s NAR-to-AR pipeline",double_column,90.00|20.00|523.65|267.87,network|pipeline,1,2,1,"Figure 2: The overview of our FSTA decomposition framework (top) and the three proposed L2Seg models (bottom). L2Seg-SYN employs a four-step synergized approach: (1) problem decomposition into subproblems, (2) unstable nodes detection globally via NAR decoding, (3) clustering of NAR- predicted nodes to localize unstable regions and select initial target nodes, and (4) refining unstable edge predictions locally via AR decoding starting from these identified initial target nodes.",68,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu,9.963,0,0,1,0,0,0,1,ArialMT|NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|Arial-BoldMT|Arial-BoldItalicMT|CambriaMath,3.848,5.199,9.963,color,6,#FFF6E4|#CCEFDC|#E9D9CD|#8C9385|#C1E4EA|#DAF2D1,0.5114,,,
