iclr-2026-ab553a040c74,ICLR,oral,replication_200,table,results,main,Table 1,7,"Hidden size, vocabulary size, ellipse sample count, and estimated sample cost for four models.",double_column,89.69|372.76|523.74|624.20,table,1,1,1,"Table 1: Models, their sizes, the number of samples required to ascertain their output ellipse, and the cost of the samples, based on OpenAI inference pricing on September 16, 2025. The number of samples required grows quadratically with the hidden size of the model, and the price grows cubically.",49,2,TeXGyreTermesX-Regular,9.963,0,1,0,0,0,0,1,TeXGyreTermesX-Regular|txsys|NewTXMI|txmiaX|NewTXBMI|NewTXMI7,7.273,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
iclr-2026-ab553a040c74,ICLR,oral,replication_200,figure,results,main,Figure 6,8,Measured solver times and degree-6 extrapolation to larger hidden dimensions.,double_column,89.42|20.00|522.00|212.45,other,1,1,1,"Figure 6: Extrapolated running time of our implementation of the ellipse extraction algorithm from Ying et al. (2012). We extract ellipses from several models with different hidden sizes (blue). Fitting a degree-6 polynomial to these points, we can extrapolate to guess at the time required to extract the parameters of larger, more popular models.",54,4,TeXGyreTermesX-Regular,9.963,0,0,0,0,0,0,0,TeXGyreTermesX-Regular|NewTXMI|NewTXMI7,2.493,8.966,9.963,color,8,#0909FB|#0404EE|#0101FB|#2B2BCA|#9D9DF3|#0101CF|#C6C6FD|#6E6EE7,0.0291,,,
iclr-2026-ab553a040c74,ICLR,oral,replication_200,figure,method,main,Figure 7,9,Standard cryptographic MAC and ellipse-based verification shown as parallel key/message/tag flows.,double_column,89.64|20.00|522.16|182.66,qualitative_grid,1,3,1,"Figure 7: A cryptographic message authentication system (left) uses a shared secret key to validate messages. The message author, Alice, signs the message by generating a tag from the message and a secret key. After receiving the message and tag, Bob verifies the message by generating another tag with his copy of the key. If the received and generated tags match, the message is authentic. In our proposed verification system (right), the ellipse functions as a secret key, and logprobs take the role of messages. The tag is encoded in the position of the logprobs in 𝑅𝑣, and can be verified by checking that they lie on the secret ellipse.",110,6,TeXGyreTermesX-Regular|NewTXMI|NewTXMI7,9.963,0,0,1,0,0,0,1,TeXGyreTermesX-Regular,5.978,8.966,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
