iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,figure,introduction,main,Figure 1,2,physical system to CI laws worked example,double_column,89.69|66.99|523.75|373.87,qualitative_grid,1,3,1,"Figure 1: Illustrative example highlighting the importance of fidelity check for tabular data structure. 1 : A real-world physical system showing the gravitational forces acting on ball A. The system is described by ball density (ρ), volume (V ), masses (m & m ), distance (r), and A B gravitational forces (F & F ). For simplicity, we assume both balls share identical density. ball Earth 2 : We derive the ground-truth (GT) causal structure of the system based on Newton’s law of universal gravitation. 3 : We interpret the encoded physical laws of the system as the conditional independence (CI) across variables. 4 : We evaluate four generators by conventional metrics. 5 : We",106,7,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMMI10,9.963,1,1,0,1,0,0,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|Calibri-Italic|Calibri-Bold|CambriaMath|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusRomNo9L-Med,1.106,3.739,14.067,color,8,#CAEEFB|#507C52|#EBA785|#8BA6A8|#B3CDD2|#F2CFC8|#E27460|#E4E5E7,0.3421,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,figure,introduction,main,Figure 2,3,"framework overview of datasets, generators, and dimensions",double_column,90.00|20.00|522.00|148.32,pipeline,1,1,1,"Figure 2: Overview of the proposed evaluation framework. TabStruct provides a comprehensive evaluation benchmark, including structural fidelity and conventional dimensions, for 13 representative tabular generative models on 29 challenging tabular datasets.",31,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,1,1,0,0,0,0,1,Calibri-Bold|Calibri|Arial-BoldMT|NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|Calibri-BoldItalic,3.594,4.972,9.963,color,8,#FFF8DC|#CAEEFB|#847A7D|#D9F2D0|#2B4A5C|#9EADB1|#E2DCC9|#C2DBCE,0.5221,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,related_work,main,Table 1,4,scope comparison with prior benchmarks,double_column,108.00|124.91|504.01|197.95,table,1,1,1,"Table 1: Evaluation scope comparison between TabStruct and prior tabular generative mod- elling benchmarks. TabStruct presents a comprehensive evaluation framework for tabular generative models, incorporating a wide range of evaluation dimensions, datasets, and generator categories.",35,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,1,1,0,0,0,0,1,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|bbding,5.13,5.13,5.13,color,8,#CCDCC6|#8CB17E|#43812B|#E73F28|#EB6451|#F9D3CE|#E2BAAB|#5D9248,0.0815,3,10,2
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,results,main,Table 2,8,13-generator aggregate results on 6 SCM and 23 real-world datasets,single_column,144.33|158.86|387.41|176.65,table,1,2,1,"Table 2: Benchmark results of 13 tabular generators on 29 datasets. We report the normalised mean std metric values across datasets. “N/A” denotes that a specific metric is not applicable. ± We highlight the First, Second and Third best performances for each metric. For visualisation, we abbreviate “conditional independence” as “CI”. The results show that the Top-3 methods in Global CI and Global utility are largely consistent between SCM and real-world datasets. This alignment suggests that the selected SCM datasets represent real-world causal structure, and global utility can serve as an effective metric for global structural fidelity when ground-truth SCM is unavailable. DensityEstimation PrivacyPreservation MLEfficacy StructuralFidelity Generator",107,7,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMSY10,9.963,1,1,1,1,0,0,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMMI10,6.143,6.143,6.143,grayscale,0,,0.0,1,3,1
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,figure,results,main,Figure 3,9,SCM correlation heatmap and real-world local/global scatter,double_column,90.00|20.00|523.74|211.30,scatter|heatmap,1,2,1,"Figure 3: Left: Spearman’s rank correlation heatmap based on metric values on six SCM datasets. Global utility correlates strongly with global CI, suggesting that global utility can effectively assess global structural fidelity without resorting to SCMs. Right: Mean normalised local utility vs. mean normalised global utility on 23 real-world datasets. SMOTE prioritises local utility, whereas TabDiff and TabSyn generally achieve a balanced preservation of both global and local data structures.",71,5,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,0,0,1,1,0,0,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|CMMI10|StandardSymL_Slant_167|CMSY10,2.515,9.299,10.487,color,8,#CFE1EC|#D56C5D|#BA2D36|#F8C9B4|#2F6296|#670120|#FBE3D6|#F2AC91,0.5538,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,figure,results,main,Figure 4,10,"training, generation, and evaluation efficiency",double_column,90.00|20.00|523.75|204.99,qualitative_grid,1,3,1,"Figure 4: Computation efficiency on 23 real-world datasets. Left: Median training time per 1,000 samples vs. mean normalised global utility. Middle: Median generation time per 1,000 samples vs. mean normalised global utility. We exclude the outliers (TabEBM and GReaT) due to their long generation time (over 30s). Right: Median evaluation time. Because global utility yields stable generator rankings across downstream predictors (Appendix E.4), computing global utility can be highly efficient with only a small ensemble of predictors (i.e., Tiny-default).",83,8,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,1,1,1,0,0,1,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMMI10|CMSY10,1.761,6.574,9.963,color,8,#F7C495|#2CA02C|#B6D5C9|#84C0A0|#717E81|#D9EAE7|#FDEBD9|#3FA63D,0.1832,,,
