iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 25,40,top-5 ranking correlations by global CI,double_column,89.67|227.85|522.00|479.29,table,1,1,1,"Table 25: Spearman’s rank correlation based on generator rankings of the top-5 generators (by global CI) on six SCM datasets. Global utility maintains a consistent ranking even among high-performing generators, showing its robustness in discerning top-performing models (p < 0.001).",41,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMR10|CMMI10,9.963,1,0,1,1,0,0,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusMonL-Regu|CMMI10|CMR10,6.023,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 26,40,global CI correlations across six and eleven SCM datasets,double_column,89.67|405.82|522.00|657.26,table,1,1,1,"Table 26: Spearman’s rank correlation with global CI on SCM datasets. We bold the highest correlation with Global CI. Global utility correlates strongly with global CI across 11 SCM datasets (p < 0.001), demonstrating the generalisability of global utility.",40,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMR10|CMMI10,9.963,1,1,0,1,0,0,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMMI10,6.023,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 27,41,absolute versus relative global utility,double_column,89.69|116.25|523.24|367.69,table,1,1,1,"Table 27: Spearman’s rank correlation with global CI on SCM datasets. We bold the highest correlation with Global CI. Global utility with normalised utility correlates strongly with global CI, showing that normalisation helps global utility to provide balanced evaluation across columns.",42,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,1,1,0,1,0,0,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMR10|CMSY10|CMMI10,9.963,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 28,41,CauTabBench metrics versus global CI,double_column,89.69|276.68|522.00|528.12,table,1,1,1,"Table 28: Spearman’s rank correlation with global CI across six SCM datasets. We bold the highest correlation with Global CI. Global utility exhibits a substantially stronger correlation with global CI compared to the CauTabBench metrics (p < 0.001), which rely on causal discovery.",44,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMR10|CMMI10,9.963,1,1,0,1,0,0,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMSY10,9.963,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 29,41,multi-table MLE metrics versus global CI,double_column,89.69|517.61|522.00|769.05,table,1,1,1,"Table 29, both MLE-R2 and MLE-F1 exhibit relatively weak correlations with global CI, suggesting that multi-table relational metrics are less suitable for evaluating inter-feature causal interactions in single-table scenarios.",29,1,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu,9.963,0,0,0,0,0,0,0,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMMI10|CMSY10|CMR10,6.974,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 30,42,synthetic sample-size ratio sensitivity,double_column,89.69|116.25|522.00|367.69,table,1,1,1,"Table 30: Global utility scores under different synthetic sample sizes. We bold the highest global utility score for each generator. In general, global utility tends to saturate when the synthetic sample size reaches or exceeds that of the reference data.",40,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,1,1,0,1,0,0,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMMI10|CMSY7|CMR10,6.974,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 31,42,GReaT versus GReaT-sort,double_column,89.69|486.88|523.25|738.32,table,1,1,1,"Table 31: Global utility of GReaT and GReaT-sort on six SCM datasets. We bold the highest performance for each dataset. GReaT-sort consistently achieves higher global utility than GReaT, indicating that aligning column order with the underlying causal structure can effectively improve the performance of autoregressive tabular models.",47,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,1,1,1,1,0,0,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMSY7,6.837,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,figure,appendix,appendix,Figure 7,43,per-feature utility distributions and three generator types,double_column,89.64|333.84|523.74|640.72,other,1,1,1,"Figure 7: Utility score distribution across features. For visual clarity, we present seven representa- tive generators and report their utility scores for each feature, accompanied by standard deviations. The results reveal that generators generally fall into three distinct categories: Generators with high global utility scores tend to exhibit balanced performance across all features. In contrast, those with relatively low global utility scores either display strong local utility or show generally poor performance across features.",74,4,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,1,1,0,1,0,0,2,Calibri-Italic|Calibri-BoldItalic,8.244,8.244,8.244,color,8,#D6E5F0|#287CB7|#C4D8E5|#76AAC5|#9BC0CD|#C05C52|#5395B8|#E89C8C,0.2265,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,figure,appendix,appendix,Figure 8,44,ranking stability under predictor pruning and tuning,double_column,89.64|267.45|523.74|574.33,other,1,2,1,"Figure 8: Comparison of ranking stability between global utility and local utility on 23 real- world datasets, evaluated using different downstream predictors. The proposed global utility produces consistent generator rankings across downstream predictors. In contrast, local utility necessitates a large set of tuned downstream predictors (i.e., Full-tuned) to yield meaningful rankings. As a result, global utility can achieve high computational efficiency with only a small ensemble of default predictors (i.e., Tiny-default in Figure 4). Global utility has the potential to evaluate global structural fidelity in the presence of latent confounders. We present a proof-of-concept experiment to investigate the behaviour of global utility on datasets containing unobserved confounders, using three SCM datasets: Sangiovese, Healthcare,",115,5,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,1,1,0,1,0,0,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal,1.42,9.963,16.806,color,8,#C5E6E0|#EEBAA0|#92B9C4|#9E6E7D|#E9E3DD|#BCD3C9|#98D4D4|#EEF2E2,0.3606,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 32,45,latent-confounder injected benchmark scores,double_column,89.69|221.92|523.25|473.36,table,1,1,1,"Table 32: Benchmark results on three SCM datasets with injected unobserved confounders, aggregated over 13 tabular generators. Global utility provides stable performance evaluations across varying numbers of unobserved confounders, demonstrating its robustness in assessing tabular data structures with latent confounding.",40,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,1,1,0,1,0,0,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMSY7|CMMI10|CMR10,5.149,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 33,45,latent-confounder global-CI correlations,double_column,89.69|380.69|522.67|632.13,table,1,1,1,Table 33: Spearman’s rank correlation with global CI (MAG) on three SCM datasets with injected unobserved confounders. We bold the highest correlation with global CI (MAG). Global utility consistently exhibits a stronger and statistically significant correlation with global CI (MAG) compared to other metrics (p < 0.001).,48,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMR10|CMMI10,9.963,1,0,0,1,0,0,1,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMMI10,5.149,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 34,46,interventional and counterfactual causal-inference errors,double_column,89.69|152.69|522.00|404.13,table,1,1,1,Table 34: Causal inference check of synthetic data from 13 tabular generators on six SCM datasets. We bold the lowest error for both interventional and counterfactual tasks. Diffusion models generally achieve the best perfomance in downstream causal inference tasks.,39,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,1,0,0,1,1,0,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|CMSY7|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMSY10,6.974,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 35,46,causal-inference metric correlations,double_column,89.69|452.27|522.00|703.71,table,1,1,1,"Table 35: Spearman’s rank correlation between causal inference metrics and other metrics on six SCM datasets. We bold the strongest correlation with causal inference performance. Global utility exhibits a strong correlation with causal inference metrics (p < 0.001), showing that global has the potential to indicate causal inference evaluations in SCM-free settings.",53,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMR10|CMMI10,9.963,1,0,0,1,0,0,1,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMMI10|CMR10|CMSY10,5.333,7.618,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 36,46,data-availability correlations,double_column,89.53|617.51|523.75|772.00,table,1,1,1,"Table36: Spearman’srankcorrelationwithglobalCIacrossdifferentdegreesofdataavailability. We bold the strongest correlation with global CI for each degree. Global utility consistently correlates strongly with global CI, showing it a stable measure for global structural fidelity given different degrees of data availability (p < 0.001).",40,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMR10|CMMI10,9.963,1,0,0,1,0,0,1,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMMI10,5.333,7.618,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
iclr-2026-ac7703f2b4ec,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 37,47,continuous/discrete metric variants,double_column,89.69|260.01|522.00|511.45,table,1,1,1,"Table 37: Spearman’s rank correlation based on generator rankings on six SCM datasets. The variants of global CI and global utility stably shows strong correlation, indicating that global utility is an effective and robust measure for global structural fidelity (p < 0.001).",43,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMR10|CMMI10,9.963,1,0,0,1,0,0,1,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,7.97,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
