iclr-2026-b7a66e10bf69,ICLR,oral,replication_200,figure,other,main,Figure 1,1,Front-page visual comparison and three-dataset average-angular-error chart; establishes RoSE's claimed overall performance and fine-detail motivation.,double_column,90.00|181.19|522.00|488.07,image_montage|map,1,2,1,"Figure 1: We present RoSE, a method using a video generative model for monocular normal map estimation, built on a new paradigm that reformulates normal estimation as a shading sequence estimation task. Results on complex and diverse scenarios show that RoSE reconstructs fine-grained geometric details and generalizes robustly to unseen datasets, achieving state-of-the-art performance in object-based monocular normal estimation on benchmark datasets.",62,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,0,1,0,1,0,0,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|ArialMT|NimbusMonL-Regu,6.304,9.963,9.963,color,8,#81B1EC|#6A94EF|#B750D9|#9895EF|#B1D6AA|#C193E1|#DDB77A|#6755CA,0.7539,,,
iclr-2026-b7a66e10bf69,ICLR,oral,replication_200,figure,introduction,main,Figure 2,2,Teapot normal-map and reconstructed-surface columns compare five methods with ground truth to make 3D misalignment visible.,double_column,90.00|20.00|522.35|226.03,conceptual_diagram|map,1,1,1,"Figure 2: Illustration of 3D misalignment. The estimated normal maps of previous methods may appear to have an overall correct appearance, yet the reconstructed surfaces often fail to align with the accurate geometry details, showing over-smooth results. In contrast, our predicted normal maps exhibit better 3D alignment, leading to more faithful surface reconstruction.",53,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,1,0,0,1,0,0,1,ArialMT|NimbusRomNo9L-Regu,6.704,6.704,9.963,color,8,#8B66F8|#C967D9|#C37CE5|#4667E5|#DB2F74|#7245E4|#6EACE1|#CCD6E4,0.6339,,,
