iclr-2026-d8ed6882cb29,ICLR,oral,replication_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 5,19,Significant feature counts for WIMHF versus ICAI in separate and joint regressions over five datasets.,double_column,90.00|160.21|522.00|411.65,table,1,1,1,"Table 5: Compared to Inverse Constitutional AI, WIMHF produces more features that statistically significantly predict preference labels. S: # of significant features when performing separate regres- sions for each method; J: # of significant features in a joint regression with both methods.",40,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,0,0,0,0,0,0,0,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|Inconsolatazi4-Regular|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMMI10|CMR10|CMSY10,8.966,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.01,,,
iclr-2026-d8ed6882cb29,ICLR,oral,replication_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 6,20,"Predictive, helpful, and interpretable counts for 47 features rated by three external ML researchers.",double_column,90.00|192.15|522.00|443.59,table,1,1,1,"Table 6: Across 5 datasets, we took the top 10 features per dataset, and first counted how many had a statistically significant prediction coefficient. Of these 47/50 features, we had expert annotators qualitatively rate them for helpfulness and interpretability. 47/47 were rated interpretable by the median of the three annotators, and 41/47 were rated helpful.",58,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu,9.963,0,1,1,0,0,0,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|Inconsolatazi4-Regular|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|CMMI10|CMMI7,6.974,9.963,11.955,color,8,#E3E7D5|#698B22|#7A983B|#94A660|#B3C48F|#CED9B7|#A2B776|#6E8D28,0.0718,,,
iclr-2026-d8ed6882cb29,ICLR,oral,replication_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 7,21,Most/least subjective CA features with dataset-level effect `βj` and random-slope spread `τj`.,double_column,90.00|319.82|521.10|571.26,table,1,1,1,"Table 7: Most and least subjective features in CA, ranked by the estimated random-slope variance τ j from the mixed-effects model. β is the dataset-level mean effect (described in §5.2). j",32,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|Inconsolatazi4-Regular|CMMI10|CMMI7,9.963,0,1,0,0,1,0,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|Inconsolatazi4-Regular,9.963,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
iclr-2026-d8ed6882cb29,ICLR,oral,replication_200,figure,appendix,appendix,Figure 4,22,"Held-out preference-prediction AUC for finetuned reward model, prompt+response embeddings, response-only embeddings, and SAE on five datasets.",double_column,90.00|20.00|522.00|298.18,line|qualitative_grid,1,3,1,"Figure 4: Human preferences are relatively well-explained by a small number of interpretable fea- tures, illustrated by the fact that using the SAE features (blue) does not perform substantially worse thananoraclefinetunedrewardmodel(grey). Notably, onlyfourfeaturesperSAEinputarenonzero, on average. Relative to random chance (AUC = 0.5), the SAE achieves 67% of the improvement realized by the reward model. This trend varies by dataset: for example, the interpretable features are highly explanatory on LMArena (93% of reward model AUC relative to random) and PRISM (77%), but there is a more substantial gap on HH-RLHF (30%), suggesting that some datasets are harder to explain with simple rules. Training a linear classifier on the full 1536-dimensional embed- dings, which the SAE is trained on, does not perform much better, averaging 77% of the full reward",129,4,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu,9.963,0,1,0,1,0,0,2,ArialMT|NimbusRomNo9L-Regu,1.709,6.05,9.963,color,5,#FC8D62|#66C2A5|#8DA0CB|#456769|#24443A,0.4917,,,
