iclr-2026-f8f364a44221,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,figure,introduction,main,Figure 1,1,"Three scaling panels compare OpenThoughts3 with AM, Nemotron Nano, LIMO, s1.1 and Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct on AIME 2025, LiveCodeBench 06/24-01/25 and GPQA Diamond.",double_column,89.67|362.40|522.00|669.28,line,1,1,1,"Figure 1: OpenThoughts3 outperforms existing SFT reasoning datasets across data scales. All models are finetuned from Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct. We compare to large SFT datasets (AM, Nemotron Nano) and small curated datasets (s1.1, LIMO) on AIME 2025 (left), LiveCodeBench 06/24-01/25 (middle), and GPQA Diamond (right). Scaling curves for all evaluation benchmarks are in Figure 6.",57,4,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,1,1,1,1,0,0,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|DejaVuSans|DejaVuSans-Bold|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,2.776,9.963,9.963,color,8,#138913|#0C0BFB|#E30621|#E14B65|#E0D4DA|#8A9ADA|#8FC395|#4251D9,0.0997,,,
iclr-2026-f8f364a44221,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,results,main,Table 1,2,"OpenThinker3-7B versus open-data 7B/8B reasoning models across math, code, science and four held-out tasks; rows report average and task accuracy.",double_column,89.69|475.27|523.74|726.71,table,1,2,1,"Table 1: OpenThinker3-7B outperforms all open-data 7B and 8B reasoning models across domains. Our model also performs well on held out benchmarks which are not measured during our main experimentation, such as HMMT and AIME25. In our ta- ble, denotes a model trained from Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct, M for Qwen-2.5-Math-Base, for Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, and for DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B. “Base Model” denotes the starting checkpoint of the training strategy. “Method” denotes the model’s optimization algorithm. In each row, we bold values within two standard errors of the highest-scoring model.",87,6,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,1,1,1,1,0,0,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu,9.564,9.963,11.955,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
iclr-2026-f8f364a44221,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,figure,method,main,Figure 2,4,"Six-stage OpenThoughts experiment pipeline from source questions through mixing, question filtering, answer generation, answer filtering and teacher selection.",double_column,90.00|20.00|522.17|148.19,pipeline|qualitative_grid,1,2,1,"Figure 2: The OpenThoughts experiment pipeline aims to build the strongest reasoning dataset recipe. We investigate (1) sourcing questions from existing and newly generated datasets, (2) mixing questions from the top-performing sources, (3) filtering for high-quality questions using fastText or LLMs, (4) deduplicating questions and sampling multiple answers per question, (5) filtering out low-quality answers using LLM verification or majority consensus, and (6) selecting the best teacher model.",68,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusMonL-Regu,9.963,1,1,1,1,0,0,3,HelveticaNeue-Bold|NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|AppleColorEmoji,8.827,8.827,29.424,color,8,#A2D8E8|#F4CD5C|#FAF2DF|#756343|#9EB9D3|#F4D79A|#A78F81|#F8E7A1,0.7333,,,
iclr-2026-f8f364a44221,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,ablation,main,Table 2,5,"Top three question sources for code, math and science, with cross-domain average and domain averages.",double_column,89.69|424.76|522.00|676.20,table,1,2,1,"Table 2: Evaluating question sources and generation strategies. We show only the top 3 scoring sources for each domain; descriptions of each source are in Appendix O.1 and full results are in Tables 31 to 33. Each row represents a unique source of questions. Question quality greatly affects performance, yielding a 17.2 gap between the strongest and the weakest code datasets. The * symbol denotes a new dataset we created with a programmatic generation strategy. Gray subscripts represent standard errors, and we bold values within two standard errors of the highest-scoring data strategy.",94,6,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMR10|CMMI10,9.963,1,1,1,1,0,1,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMR7|CMMI7,6.974,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
iclr-2026-f8f364a44221,ICLR,oral,foundation_200,table,ablation,main,Table 3,5,"Code source-mixing ablation for top 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 sources.",double_column,89.69|584.83|522.00|772.00,table,1,1,1,Table 3: Mixing different code question sources. Our experiments show that choosing only the two best question sources outperforms mixing more question sources. Similar results hold for science and math data domains. Full results including the math and science datasets are in Tables 34 to 36.,46,4,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,9.963,1,0,0,1,0,0,1,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu,9.963,9.963,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
