icml-2026-07e2bc33888c,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,figure,introduction,main,Figure 1,2,"Overview of clean RR and utility/safety poisoning paths from reference to proliferation, fine-tuning, and user classification.",page_width,36.89|20.00|559.67|240.06,pipeline|qualitative_grid,1,3,1,"Figure 1. Overview of Rapid Response (RR) and poisoning attack vectors. The clean pipeline (top row) collects in-the-wild jailbreaks as references, proliferates them into synthetic training samples, and fine-tunes a guard model that correctly classifies these new jailbreak strategies as unsafe. Our poisoning attacks can be used for two practical objectives (two bottom rows): (i) Utility degradation: we poison the model to misclassify a subpopulation of safe queries (here, queries with the word “ChatGPT”) as unsafe. (ii) Safety degradation: we introduce a more sophisticated Omission Attack that creates a backdoor which can evade detection when added to unsafe inputs. This will be explained in detail in Section 3.3 and Figure 3.",112,6,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|LMMono9-Regular,8.966,0,1,1,0,0,0,2,NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,8.966,8.966,8.966,color,8,#FFF2CC|#6D7C8E|#EFC2A0|#FFE8CE|#ABAFB7|#536076|#E9DFC8|#FFF2D6,0.4046,,,
