icml-2026-19ec66cff1c1,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,figure,appendix,appendix,Figure 79,62,Quadratic-drift example of P-control oscillation at lr=0.05.,page_width,67.04|302.49|544.96|609.37,line|qualitative_grid,1,2,1,"Figure 79: UP-OCP vs. P Ctrl (lr=0.05) on synthetic data with quadratic trend and random waves; While the parameter-free UP-OCP (red) maintains stable coverage near the target, the P Controller (purple) exhibits significant oscillation. Intuitively this indicates that the controller is over-reacting to single data points.",48,3,CMR10,9.963,0,0,0,1,0,0,1,ArialMT|CMR10,1.544,6.963,9.963,color,8,#E3382F|#EC4537|#8E2577|#EA7168|#B67A81|#F7E6DA|#EFCEB9|#E3453B,0.2036,,,
icml-2026-19ec66cff1c1,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,figure,appendix,appendix,Figure 80,63,Synthetic-sinusoid example where PI-control lr=0.0 does not adapt to periodicity.,page_width,67.04|20.00|544.96|205.86,qualitative_grid,1,2,1,"Figure 80: UP-OCP vs. PI Ctrl (lr=0.0) on synthetic sinusoid data; PI Controller fails to adapt to periodicity of the ground truth (black). The prediction set sizes (cyan, right panel) remain effectively constant. The coverage (left panel) oscillates deterministically between 0% and 100% as the ground truth noise wave passes in and out. In contrast, UP-OCP (red) correctly modulates the interval width to track the sinusoidal pattern, maintaining valid coverage.",72,5,CMR10,9.963,0,0,1,1,1,0,3,ArialMT,1.544,6.087,6.963,color,8,#26C6DB|#EC4638|#E89790|#E4F5F7|#73DAE8|#BCEEF4|#95342D|#55CEDE,0.4304,,,
icml-2026-1c5182bdf0c8,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,figure,method,main,Figure 1,2,"Full-width conceptual workflow: base generation, thinking-model classifier, and category-vector steering; arrows and colored category boxes encode the what/when decomposition.",page_width,36.89|46.61|559.75|353.49,pipeline|conceptual_diagram,3,3,1,"Figure 1. Constructive Model Diffing for Thinking LLMs. We decompose the difference between base and thinking models into two interpretable components. (A) The base model generates tokens, providing the underlying reasoning capabilities. (B) A reasoning heuristic, extracted from the thinking model via SAE activations, determines when to deploy each reasoning mechanism. (C) Category vectors, optimized in the base model, induce the corresponding reasoning behavior when triggered. If this explicit construction recovers the thinking model’s performance, it provides evidence that the diff is well-characterized by our decomposition: the thinking model learned sophisticated heuristics over pre-existing base model mechanisms.",98,7,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal,8.966,0,1,0,0,0,0,1,unknown|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,7.42,9.275,16.695,color,8,#CDBCF3|#8FDAB3|#E6AECA|#E8D8B0|#6B4D61|#D5C6F7|#AE91AF|#C0B5E3,0.5384,,,
icml-2026-1c5182bdf0c8,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,figure,results,main,Figure 2,4,Full-width 9-panel grid search across layer and cluster size; color encodes combined normalized taxonomy score and outlines top/bottom configurations.,page_width,36.89|20.00|561.01|321.64,other,1,2,1,"Figure 2. Grid search over SAE taxonomies. Combined score (average of completeness, independence, and consistency) across layers (x-axis) and cluster sizes (y-axis, 5–50), for DeepSeek-R1 SFT-distilled variants and QwQ-32B (top) and Open-Reasoner-Zero models (bottom). The three best configurations per model are outlined in green, the three worst in red. While scores rise with cluster size, an “elbow” at 10–20 categories indicates reasoning mechanisms are already well represented at that scale in aggregate; individual models’ elbows can sit lower, so the per-model taxonomies we ultimately select (Appendix G) use dictionary sizes in the 5–15 range. The colorbar’s “F1 · completeness · orthogonality” label denotes this same combined score, where “F1” and “orthogonality” are the consistency and independence metrics defined in Section 2.3; the three components are averaged, not multiplied.",131,6,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMR9|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|CMSY9,8.966,0,1,1,1,0,1,3,DejaVuSans|DejaVuSans-Bold|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,1.135,3.867,8.966,color,8,#D3DEEC|#B5C8E1|#8B9EBD|#95B0D4|#A4BADA|#F1E0D8|#857480|#C8BBC2,0.7444,,,
icml-2026-1c5182bdf0c8,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,figure,results,main,Figure 3,6,"Training-epoch line curves for validation, MATH-500, and GSM8K holdout CE; colors distinguish ORZ, R1, and QwQ and stars mark best validation epoch.",page_width,36.89|20.00|559.75|257.50,line,1,1,1,"Figure 3. Category vector training: holdout loss vs. epoch. RL-trained ORZ models (warm colours) converge to lower cross-entropy than SFT-distilled R1 models (cool colours) across all three held-out sets, indicating that ORZ-derived category vectors find more causally effective directions in the base model. The mixed-training QwQ model falls in between. Stars mark the best epoch per model on the validation split.",61,6,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal,8.966,1,1,0,1,0,0,2,DejaVuSans|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,2.238,7.102,8.966,color,8,#93C0D7|#5998C7|#C7E1DF|#D04642|#F69B8B|#F5E0E0|#E8EEEE|#364B80,0.4427,,,
icml-2026-1c5182bdf0c8,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,table,results,main,Table 1,7,"Primary base/hybrid/thinking accuracy triplets and Rec.% across nine pairs and three benchmarks, with mean plus/minus std.",page_width,36.94|127.78|561.01|379.22,table,1,2,1,"Table 1. Constructive model diffing results on GSM8K, MATH500, and a held-out Hendrycks-MATH subset. We compare each base, thinking, and hybrid model triplet. Base and hybrid models decode greedily; thinking models sample 3 rollouts at temperature 0.6. All use a 2048-token budget. Accuracy is graded by Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Anthropic, 2025), which agrees closely with human grading (κ = 0.88; Appendix C); we run 3 judge repetitions per rollout and report mean±std over all rollout-judge pairs. The Hendrycks-MATH set is a 1,000-question subset of Hendrycks et al. (2021), disjoint from both the training mix and MATH500. Rec.% is the fraction of the base-to-thinking gap that the hybrid recovers",112,8,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMR9|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|CMMI9,8.966,1,1,0,0,0,1,1,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|CMR9|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|CMMI9|CMSY9,4.71,7.065,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0102,,,
icml-2026-1c5182bdf0c8,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,table,results,main,Table 2,7,"Steered-token fraction by pair and benchmark, supporting the sparse-intervention claim.",single_column,24.00|317.97|301.00|569.41,table,1,1,1,Table 2. Steered token fraction. Average fraction of tokens new ones. receiving steering per problem.,15,4,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal,8.966,0,0,0,0,0,0,0,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,7.013,7.013,9.963,grayscale,0,,0.0,,,
icml-2026-1c5182bdf0c8,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,figure,ablation,main,Figure 4,8,"Grouped bars compare full pipeline with random category, random vectors, MLP magnitude only, and random positions for two ORZ pairs.",page_width,36.89|20.00|561.09|238.05,bar|pipeline,1,2,1,"Figure 4. Negative-control ablations. We run hybrid evalua- Venhoff et al. (2025) identify interpretable activation vec- tion on MATH500 and GSM8K for Open-Reasoner-Zero-1.5B tors for reasoning behaviors, and Ward et al. (2025a) dis- and -32B while ablating various hybrid-model components. “Full cover directions in base models that steer reasoning. Ward pipeline” bars are the baseline gap-recovery results; every other et al. (2025b) show that rank-1 LoRA adapters can recover bar removes a single component. All ablations sharply reduce 73–90% of reasoning performance with minimal parameter recovery, confirming that each component contributes. changes.",94,10,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|CMR10,8.966,0,1,1,1,0,0,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|DejaVuSans|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|DejaVuSans-Bold,2.889,9.963,9.963,color,8,#547FAC|#D1D1DE|#B4B2C5|#C13F31|#C0392B|#486EA3|#9A91AC|#AA5C65,0.2059,,,
icml-2026-1c5182bdf0c8,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,table,appendix,appendix,Table 3,13,Five-seed ORZ-7B SAE feature matching and cosine stability diagnostic.,page_width,36.94|533.13|559.44|772.00,table,1,1,1,"Table 3. SAE feature stability across random seeds for the ORZ-7B SAE (layer 16, dictionary size 15). Features are matched one-to-one via the Hungarian algorithm on cosine similarity; a pair counts as matched when cosine > 0.7 (Paulo & Belrose, 2026).",40,3,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|CMR9|CMMI9,8.966,0,1,0,0,0,0,1,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|CMR9|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|CMMI9|CMR10|CMBX9,8.966,8.966,9.963,color,8,#12237C|#A2A9CC|#394791|#5360A0|#BBC0D9|#8891BD|#737EB2|#CBCFE2,0.032,,,
icml-2026-1c5182bdf0c8,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,figure,appendix,appendix,Figure 5,18,A qualitative Qwen2.5-32B hybrid MATH500 trace with color-coded steering categories at token positions.,page_width,36.89|352.69|561.01|659.57,qualitative_grid,1,3,1,"Figure 5. Hybrid model in action. Example of a hybrid model (Qwen2.5-32B as base model with category vectors trained on Open- Reasoner-Zero-32B) solving a MATH500 problem. Each token in the model’s response is highlighted according to the SAE category that triggered steering at that position. The model is steered at ~12% of token positions, spanning six distinct reasoning categories (Performing Calculations and Problem Decomposition most prominent). After an initial estimate it self-verifies (“this seems to be an underestimation. Let’s manually verify ...and see if we can find a better solution”), then searches over candidate values and arrives at the correct answer 17, while the unsteered base model fails.",111,7,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|CMR9,8.966,0,1,0,0,0,0,1,,,,,color,8,#F9E2B6|#C5DFF1|#A8A9A7|#C0E0EF|#7B797B|#CDE6F3|#EBCECA|#B8D2DE,0.1444,,,
