icml-2026-67206cc61e49,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,figure,introduction,main,Figure 1,2,"Full-width CPC pipeline from safe prompts and observed data through policy improvement, user alpha, calibration, and accept–reject deployment; colors separate safe, optimized, and constrained policies.",page_width,36.89|20.00|559.44|177.30,pipeline|conceptual_diagram,1,3,1,"Figure 1. An illustration of safe exploration with conformal policy control. Starting with a safe policy π and safe context prompts 0 X := {x ,...,x }, we observe the safe policy’s actions a and corresponding reward r ∈ R and constraint violation ℓ ∈ {0,1}. We 0 1 m i i i split the observations into train and calibration data, and optimize the safe policy to obtain π . We then query the user for their constraint t violation risk tolerance α, and apply conformal risk control to find a bound on the likelihood ratio π /π which guarantees the calibrated t 0 risk is controlled at level α. Finally we apply rejection sampling to probabilistically regulate the optimized policy for deployment and",121,5,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|CMMI9|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|CMSY9|CMMI6,8.966,0,0,0,0,0,0,0,HelveticaNeue-Bold|STIXGeneral-Italic|STIXGeneral-Regular|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,5.21,6.075,15.188,color,8,#EB9793|#AEDAD6|#C2E2E0|#4E618B|#99A4B3|#DCE9E9|#DAD0D9|#A9D2D3,0.5458,,,
icml-2026-67206cc61e49,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,figure,related_work,main,Figure 2,4,"Two-panel synthetic comparison of standard CRC and gCRC under non-monotonic losses; axes show target risk, realized test risk, and empirical-risk trajectory.",single_column,311.00|20.00|588.00|179.48,line|qualitative_grid,1,2,1,"Figure 2. Empirical comparison of standard CRC (Angelopoulos et al., 2024) (orange) and the proposed generalized CRC (blue) on synthetic non-monotonic losses. gCRC achieves risk control 0.4 0.6 0.8 1.0 across various target risk levels while CRC does not (left). On an example empirical risk trajectory for these experiments, CRC underestimates the risk while gCRC maintains risk control by searching from safe to aggressive hyperparameter values (right).",71,4,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal,8.966,0,1,1,1,0,0,3,DejaVuSans|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,1.316,4.016,8.966,color,8,#296AAC|#F8A257|#FF7F0E|#8AA3BD|#537BA5|#FF861B|#DEC5B0|#E6DAD0,0.2086,,,
icml-2026-67206cc61e49,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,figure,method,main,Figure 3,5,Three beta panels visualize density interpolation and accepted/rejected samples for rejection sampling.,page_width,36.89|20.00|560.56|162.00,bar|qualitative_grid,3,4,1,"Figure 3. Rejection sampling for policy interpolation at three β values. As β increases from near-zero to large values, the constrained policy π(β) interpolates from the safe policy π to the optimized policy π . Blue histogram bars show accepted samples matching π(β), t 0 t t while teal/red bars show rejected samples from the proposal distribution. (a) Small β nearly recovers the safe policy (b) Intermediate β shows intermediate interpolation with frequent rejections. (c) Large β approaches the optimized policy with minimal rejection.",86,6,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|CMMI9|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|CMR6|CMMI6,8.966,0,0,0,1,0,0,1,ArialMT|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|DejaVuSans-Oblique|CMR9|CMMI9,1.156,5.727,8.966,color,8,#A6C2DD|#F3C2BF|#779DBB|#37669A|#D4EBE9|#F6CCCA|#C1A4AF|#AFD3DE,0.43,,,
icml-2026-67206cc61e49,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,figure,results,main,Figure 4,7,Medical-QA FDR versus target alpha and true-claim recall versus alpha; dashed diagonal encodes target-risk comparison and standard-error regions encode 25 random splits.,single_column,311.00|20.00|588.00|184.19,line,1,1,1,Figu0r.e064. M0e.0d8ical QA factuality control results on MedLFQA. pLeer fBto:unEdm (p)irical FDR (fraction of retained claims that are false) vs. target risk level α. The dashed line indicates y = x; valid methods should fall at or below this line. Right: Recall (fraction of true claims retained) vs. α. gCRC tightly controls FDR at the target level while achieving superior recall to baselines. Results averaged over 25 random splits. Error regions are standard errors.,77,10,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|CMMI9|CMR9,8.966,0,0,1,1,1,0,3,DejaVuSans|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,1.53,3.754,8.966,color,8,#946258|#427FA9|#49A35E|#BEB7B9|#DAE5DF|#AA837B|#67B86C|#B7DABF,0.2578,,,
icml-2026-67206cc61e49,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,figure,results,main,Figure 5,8,"Three-dataset active-learning trajectories: constraint-violation rate and holdout MSE for CPC, uncontrolled, and Conformal Controller.",page_width,36.89|20.00|560.56|235.75,matrix,1,3,1,"0.0 0 10 20 30 40 50 60 70 Number of Active Learning Steps t noC Figure 5. Conformal policy control in the active learning setting. We train Gaussian process regression models on tabular datasets, providing a small amount of initial data for training and selecting the remaining training data via exponential tilting toward the posterior predictive variance. We introduce a feasibility constraint based on alignment with the leading principal component of the Gram matrix to make the task more difficult. CPC controls the constraint violation risk at our desired threshold α = 0.2, while simultaneously reducing test MSE. Surprisingly, in some cases the risk-controlled data selection policy attains lower test MSE than the uncontrolled policy.",117,6,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|CMR9|CMMI9,8.966,0,1,0,1,1,0,3,DejaVuSans|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|DejaVuSans-Oblique,1.075,4.387,9.963,color,8,#2166AB|#E6E4C1|#C7C45F|#D8332A|#3A6197|#5384A9|#DB4B42|#CDA078,0.4042,,,
icml-2026-67206cc61e49,ICML,spotlight,foundation_200,figure,results,main,Figure 6,9,"Constrained black-box sequence-optimization trajectories for infeasibility, average score, and maximum score across risk levels.",page_width,36.89|20.00|559.66|189.52,box|qualitative_grid,1,3,1,"Figure 6. We0a.p0pl0y CPC to5constrain10ed black1-5box sequence optimization0 scored w5ith Ehrl1ic0h functi1o5ns. Left: average infeasib0ility rate5per round. Center: averag Ti e m o e b st j e e p c t o iv f e Po v li a c l y u I e m o p f ro s v e e q m u e e n n t ces sampled from the poli T c i y m . eRstiegph ot:f P b o e li s c t y o Im bj p e r c o t v i e v m e e v n a t lue found so far. CPC allow Tim s e d s i t r e e p c t o control over constraint violation risk via α. Without CPC, the infeasibility risk quickly rises to over 80% of policy samples. Moderate risk control (α > 0.6) stabilizes optimization and improves overall performance by reducing wasted samples on infeasible actions.",165,8,NimbusRomNo9L-Regu|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi|NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal|CMMI9|CMR9,8.966,0,0,1,1,0,0,2,DejaVuSans|NimbusRomNo9L-Medi,1.541,4.964,8.966,color,8,#E9E0F1|#DCC0D0|#667BB7|#D8F4F7|#75BED4|#F2D9DD|#CD7383|#DAE0EF,0.5247,,,
