icml-2026-ca6b03466916,ICML,oral,foundation_200,figure,results,main,Figure 2,8,"Compares ImageNet training dynamics for exact ML, MaxRL, and REINFORCE.",page_width,52.87|20.00|542.41|193.44,image_montage,1,2,1,"Figure 2: (ImageNet) Comparison of training dynamics under exact maximum likelihood, MaxRL, and REINFORCE in a controlled image classification setting. With sufficient rollouts, MaxRL closely matches cross-entropy training, while REINFORCE fails to make progress from low initial pass rates.",39,2,SFRM0800|SFBX0800|SFCC0800,7.97,0,1,0,0,0,0,1,DejaVuSans,1.736,6.245,7.584,color,8,#FE976C|#FBC17F|#F5D8AB|#4D728F|#9C979D|#F3E2C9|#BB483C|#F7ECD9,0.1198,,,
icml-2026-ca6b03466916,ICML,oral,foundation_200,figure,results,main,Figure 3,8,Shows maze pass@k scaling over training-rollout budgets.,page_width,52.87|263.85|542.41|590.19,line,1,2,1,"Figure 3: (Maze) Motivated by Schaeffer et al. (2025), we record −log(pass@k) (lower is better) as a function of training rollouts for different objectives. We see that across all different inference rollout budgets (k), MaxRL exhibits better scaling compared to GRPO and RLOO as we increase number of training rollouts. Moreover, there is almost no gap in pass@k and pass@1 for GRPO and RLOO despite no repeated data, indicating that these algorithms may have lost the ability to improve further, whereas MaxRL has a substantial gap and potentially can improve more given additional compute.",98,4,SFRM0800|CMR8|SFBX0800|SFCC0800|CMMI8,7.97,0,1,0,1,0,0,2,SFRM1000|DejaVuSerif|SFTI1000|DejaVuSans|SFCC1000|SFBX1000,2.103,9.963,9.963,color,8,#73AD70|#FE5F4D|#51768C|#B1CBB9|#EFD2D0|#5C4545|#6D8B9D|#F58175,0.0493,,,
icml-2026-ca6b03466916,ICML,oral,foundation_200,figure,results,main,Figure 4,9,Shows GSM8K pass@k trajectories through extended fixed-data training.,page_width,52.87|74.60|542.63|400.93,other,1,1,1,Figure 4: (GSM8K) Training dynamics on GSM8K with a fixed dataset and increasing training compute in terms of RL steps. MaxRL shows slower initial gains but ultimately achieves higher performance with substantially less pass@k degradation.,36,2,SFRM0800|SFBX0800|SFCC0800,7.97,0,1,0,1,0,0,2,SFRM1000|DejaVuSerif|SFTI1000|SFCC1000|SFBX1000|CMR10,2.435,9.963,9.963,color,8,#597E91|#79AF79|#FC5D4C|#BDD2C5|#F4D3CF|#FB887B|#89929E|#4F515B,0.046,,,
icml-2026-ca6b03466916,ICML,oral,foundation_200,figure,results,main,Figure 5,10,Reports final Qwen3 coverage curves on four math benchmarks.,page_width,52.56|20.00|543.58|288.54,qualitative_grid,1,3,1,"Figure 5: (Qwen3 training results) Evaluation of final checkpoints from training Qwen3-1.7B-Base and Qwen3-4B-Base, on 4 benchmarks: AIME 2025, BeyondAIME, MATH-500 and Minerva. MaxRL matches or outperforms GRPO in all 4 evaluation datasets and shows little to no degradation at coverage (pass@k) for very high k values. We also note the increase in inference efficiency: MaxRL can provide 2.3× - 19.2× speedup compared to GRPO while generating multiple samples with a perfect verifier and maintains similar or better pass@1 performance.",84,3,SFRM0800|SFBX0800|SFCC0800|CMR8|CMMI8,7.97,0,1,0,1,0,0,2,DejaVuSans|DejaVuSans-Bold,2.485,6.252,8.753,color,8,#78B172|#F55F4E|#5E3D33|#B6D3B4|#F3CCC7|#FD7364|#FB9084|#F3E2DF,0.043,,,
icml-2026-ca6b03466916,ICML,oral,foundation_200,figure,ablation,main,Figure 6,11,Relates per-prompt gradient norm to pass rate for three objectives.,page_width,52.87|20.00|542.70|144.83,scatter,1,1,1,"Figure 6: (Gradient norm analysis) To compare different objectives qualitatively, we show a scatter plot of gradient L2 norm vs pass rate over individual prompts. We use Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct on MATH-500 dataset for this analysis. MaxRL generates larger gradient norms prompts with close to 0 pass rates.",48,3,SFRM0800|SFBX0800|SFCC0800|CMMI8|CMR6,7.97,0,1,0,1,0,0,2,DejaVuSans|DejaVuSans-Oblique,1.716,5.145,7.203,color,8,#9CB9AB|#F6B7B0|#CAD6C1|#ACCBAC|#E3E1D8|#CFBFC3|#D6DCCD|#BE8384,0.113,,,
icml-2026-ca6b03466916,ICML,oral,foundation_200,figure,ablation,main,Figure 7,11,Tracks the fraction of training prompts with non-zero pass rate.,page_width,51.88|20.00|542.63|292.99,other,1,1,1,"Figure 7: (Training dynamics comparison) Fraction of prompts where the model generates at least one correct rollout (out of 128, 16, and 16 rollouts for SmolLM-360M-Instruct, Qwen3-1.7B-Base, and Qwen3-4B-Base, respectively) during training. MaxRL consistently produces at least one correct rollout for more prompts.",44,2,SFRM0800|SFBX0800|SFCC0800,7.97,0,1,0,1,0,0,2,DejaVuSans|SFRM0800|SFBX0800|SFCC0800|DejaVuSans-Oblique|CMMI8,1.716,7.053,7.97,color,8,#FE6757|#7BAF7D|#E5B0AC|#BCD5B9|#A1C4A2|#F0C9C6|#B7C9C5|#E4E4DB,0.084,,,
icml-2026-ca6b03466916,ICML,oral,foundation_200,figure,appendix,appendix,Figure 8,42,"Compares ImageNet gradient norms across CE, MaxRL, GRPO, and REINFORCE.",page_width,52.44|20.00|544.05|302.81,scatter|qualitative_grid|image_montage,1,3,1,"Figure 8: (ImageNet gradient norm analysis) Scatter plot, where each point has the pass rate (model’s predicted probability of the correct class) of a particular image in the x-axis, and gradient L2 norm for that image in the y-axis, for 1000 randomly selected images from the ImageNet validation dataset after 1500 steps of training on a ResNet-50 model. Sampling-based algorithms’ (MaxRL, GRPO and REINFORCE) gradients are calculated using 131,072 rollouts per example to reduce sampling error and estimate the population-level gradient. Cross-entropy and MaxRL have similar scatter plot: with high gradient norm for hard inputs (pass rate close to 0) and lower gradient norm for the easier ones (pass rate close to 1). In contrast, the highest gradient norm for GRPO is on medium-difficulty (pass rate close to 0.5) inputs, with hard inputs having very low gradient norm. Finally, REINFORCE fails to produce any significant gradient norm and its pass rate is confined below 0.003 after 1500 steps, demonstrating its difficulty to learn in this setting.",170,5,SFRM0800|SFBX0800|SFCC0800|CMMI8|CMR6,7.97,0,1,1,1,1,0,4,SFRM1000|DejaVuSans|CMR10|SFBX1000|CMMI10|CMSY7,2.145,9.963,9.963,color,8,#CED5C8|#A8C5B3|#86A7A8|#E6C6C5|#607F9E|#EAAAA5|#FA8579|#DCE1DA,0.0824,,,
icml-2026-ca6b03466916,ICML,oral,foundation_200,figure,appendix,appendix,Figure 9,42,"Adds ImageNet GRPO curves, majority voting, and pass@k coverage.",page_width,52.55|267.81|542.72|594.14,image_montage,1,3,1,"Figure 9: (Additional ImageNet results) On the didactic image classification setting, MaxRL outperforms and scales better than GRPO with additional compute, and approaches the same performance as maximum likelihood training via cross-entropy given sufficient number of rollouts (≥ 1024). Note that REINFORCE remains flat, since the initial model’s pass rate is low (∼ 0.1%) and REINFORCE fails to generate significant gradient signal (Figure 8). From left, the plots show pass@1, pass@128, Majority Voting Accuracy (equivalent to traditional validation top-1 accuracy in image classification, see Appendix H.3), and coverage of the final checkpoint, respectively.",96,3,SFRM0800|SFBX0800|CMR8|SFCC0800|CMSY8,7.97,0,1,1,1,0,1,3,SFRM0800|SFRM1000|DejaVuSans|SFBX0800|SFCC0800|SFCC1000,1.359,7.97,9.963,color,8,#FA9D7E|#7D9E8F|#EAE9D1|#F5C5B6|#BDCA54|#92BC8B|#AA4E43|#C5D697,0.0725,,,
icml-2026-ca6b03466916,ICML,oral,foundation_200,figure,appendix,appendix,Figure 10,44,Shows one successful and one failed maze trajectory.,page_width,52.87|359.75|542.64|686.09,line|qualitative_grid,1,2,1,"Figure 10: (Maze data visualization) The left plot shows a successful navigation trajectory, while the right plot illustrates a failure case produced by the trained model, where the generated action sequence deviates from the correct path before reaching the goal.",40,1,SFRM0800|SFBX0800,7.97,0,1,1,1,0,0,3,Inconsolatazi4-Regular|SFRM1000|SFBX0900|SFBX1000,8.966,8.966,9.963,color,8,#374859|#475261|#39495A|#838C92|#2E4051|#374657|#374858|#364859,0.3768,,,
icml-2026-ca6b03466916,ICML,oral,foundation_200,figure,appendix,appendix,Figure 11,45,Tracks high-budget maze training dynamics.,page_width,52.56|251.14|542.64|577.48,line,1,2,1,"Figure 11: (Training dynamics in the maze setting under our highest compute budget) We investigate how the Pass@k performance of different objectives evolve when we train a 3M model to solve 17x17 maze puzzles on our highest compute budget, namely 256 batch size and 128 training rollouts per task. MaxRL performs significantly better compared with GRPO and REINFORCE in pass@1, pass@32, pass@128 and pass@256. These results signify MaxRL’s effectiveness in compute scaling during RL.",80,3,SFRM0800|SFBX0800|SFCC0800,7.97,0,1,0,1,0,0,2,SFRM1000|DejaVuSerif|SFBX1000|SFCC1000|Inconsolatazi4-Regular,2.053,9.963,9.963,color,8,#6085A7|#B7CDCA|#7E9BBA|#7DAF88|#3E576C|#D95547|#DFE4E8|#E88E84,0.0812,,,
icml-2026-ca6b03466916,ICML,oral,foundation_200,figure,appendix,appendix,Figure 12,46,Compares maze curves with additional baselines.,page_width,52.87|20.00|543.59|196.99,other,1,1,1,"Figure 12: (Training curves compared with other baselines.) We compare MaxRL with other RL algorithms, including entropy bonus, PKPO (Walder and Karkhanis, 2025), Differential Smoothing (Gai et al., 2026) and SELF (Nguyen et al., 2025b), under batch size 32 and number of training rollouts 128. MaxRL significantly outperforms other methods in all metrics, and is the only method to maintain both good average performance (pass@1) and coverage (pass@k).",70,2,SFRM0800|SFBX0800|SFCC0800,7.97,0,1,0,1,0,0,2,DejaVuSerif|SFBX1000,2.504,5.253,9.963,color,8,#EA986F|#344B5A|#7E8DA0|#A7BEA3|#B1D8CB|#E8D7C3|#A45A7E|#61C5B6,0.1192,,,
